# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Solution blocks inserted under each task of the Lab 9 Solutions document (Lab 7/8 Solutions format)."""


def head(given, find, concept):
    return [('p', '**Solution.**'),
            ('p', f'**What is given?** {given}'),
            ('p', f'**What do we need to find?** {find}'),
            ('p', f'**Which concept applies?** {concept}')]


S = {}
S[1] = head('n = 15 developers; X = years of experience, Y = annual salary in $1000s; the sums x̄, ȳ, Σ(x − x̄)², Σ(x − x̄)(y − ȳ) and SST; α = 0.05.',
            'the regression line, its interpretation, R², the significance of the slope and a prediction.',
            'simple linear regression with least squares, R² = SSR / SST and the t-test for the slope (df = n − 2 = 13).') + [
 ('p', '**a)** We want to predict the salary from the experience, so X = years of experience (independent) and Y = salary (dependent). The scatter plot shows a strong, positive, linear pattern: developers with more experience earn more.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: X = years of experience, Y = salary; a strong positive linear relationship.'),
 ('p', '**b)** Using the least squares formulas:'),
 ('eq', r'$$b_1 = \frac{804.95}{134.04} = 6.005 \qquad b_0 = 61.753 - 6.005 \cdot 5.453 = 29.00 \qquad \hat{y} = 29.00 + 6.005x$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: ŷ = 29.00 + 6.005x (=SLOPE → 6.0054, =INTERCEPT → 29.0037).'),
 ('p', '**c)** Every extra year of experience increases the expected salary by about **$6,005** per year. The intercept 29.00 is the predicted salary of a developer with 0 years of experience (about $29,000). It is close to the data (the smallest X is 1.2 years), so it can be read as a rough starting salary, but no developer in the sample has exactly 0 years.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: +$6,005 per year of experience; starting salary of about $29,000.'),
 ('p', '**d)** The explained variation and R² are:'),
 ('eq', r'$$SSR = b_1 \cdot \sum (x - \bar{x})(y - \bar{y}) = 6.005 \cdot 804.95 = 4834.1 \qquad R^2 = \frac{4834.1}{5046.71} = 0.958$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: R² = 0.958; experience explains about 95.8% of the variation in salaries.'),
 ('p', '**e)** In the Excel output, the slope has t Stat = 17.19 and P-value = 2.5 · 10⁻¹⁰ < 0.05. The 95% confidence interval for β₁ is [5.25, 6.76] and does not contain 0.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: we reject H₀: β₁ = 0. Experience has a significant effect on salary; each year adds between $5,250 and $6,760.'),
 ('p', '**f)** For x = 5: ŷ = 29.00 + 6.005 · 5 = 59.03, i.e. about $59,000. For 40 years the model would give 29.00 + 6.005 · 40 = 269.2 ($269,000).'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: about $59,000 for 5 years. A prediction for 40 years is extrapolation: the data only go from 1.2 to 9.7 years, and salaries do not keep growing linearly for a whole career.'),
 ('p', '**Excel check:** =SLOPE(B2:B16, A2:A16) → 6.0054, =INTERCEPT(…) → 29.0037, =RSQ(…) → 0.9579, =FORECAST.LINEAR(5, …) → 59.03.'),
]
S[2] = head('n = 15 cars of the same model; X = age in years, Y = price in 1000 KM; the sums; α = 0.05.',
            'the regression line, the yearly loss of value, R², the F-test and two predictions.',
            'simple linear regression, R² and the F-test (Significance F) in the ANOVA table.') + [
 ('p', '**a)** X = age (we use it to predict), Y = price. The points go down from left to right: the relationship is **negative** and close to linear.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: X = age, Y = price; a strong negative relationship.'),
 ('p', '**b)** The slope and the intercept are:'),
 ('eq', r'$$b_1 = \frac{-483.13}{238.93} = -2.022 \qquad b_0 = 19.107 - (-2.022) \cdot 8.267 = 35.82 \qquad \hat{y} = 35.82 - 2.022x$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: ŷ = 35.82 − 2.022x.'),
 ('p', '**c)** Every year the car loses on average **2.022 thousand KM**, i.e. about 2,020 KM of its value. The intercept 35.82 is the predicted price of a “0-year-old” car (about 35,800 KM). It is a rough estimate of the price of a new car, but the data start at 2 years, and a new car usually loses a lot of value in the first year, so this number should be used with care.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: about −2,020 KM per year; b₀ ≈ 35,800 KM is only a rough “new car” price.'),
 ('p', '**d)** SSR = b₁ · Σ(x − x̄)(y − ȳ) = (−2.022) · (−483.13) = 976.9, so R² = 976.9 / 1035.15 = 0.944.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: R² = 0.944; age explains 94.4% of the variation in prices. The rest comes from mileage, equipment, condition, accident history, etc.'),
 ('p', '**e)** The ANOVA table gives F = 217.98 with Significance F = 1.7 · 10⁻⁹, and the P-value of the slope is the same number (t = −14.76, and t² = 218 = F).'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: Significance F < 0.05, so we reject H₀: β₁ = 0. Age has a significant effect on price.'),
 ('p', '**f)** For x = 6: ŷ = 35.82 − 2.022 · 6 = 23.69, i.e. about 23,690 KM. The ad asks 20,000 KM, about 3,700 KM less than the model predicts. For x = 20: ŷ = 35.82 − 2.022 · 20 = −4.62.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: the 6-year-old car is about 3,700 KM cheaper than predicted (a good deal, or a sign of a hidden problem). A negative price for a 20-year-old car is impossible: it is extrapolation beyond the data (2 to 15 years).'),
 ('p', '**Excel check:** =SLOPE(…) → −2.0220, =INTERCEPT(…) → 35.8220, =RSQ(…) → 0.9437, =FORECAST.LINEAR(6, …) → 23.69.'),
]
S[3] = head('n = 15 apps; X = average rating, Y = number of downloads; the sums; α = 0.05.',
            'the regression line, Multiple R, Adjusted R², the standard error s, the significance of the slope and the meaning of the intercept.',
            'simple linear regression and the Regression Statistics part of the Excel output.') + [
 ('p', '**a)** The points show an upward trend, but they are much more spread around the line than in Tasks 1 and 2: the relationship is positive but **weak to moderate**.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: a positive but much weaker relationship than in Tasks 1 and 2.'),
 ('p', '**b)** The slope and the intercept are:'),
 ('eq', r'$$b_1 = \frac{13458.79}{7.197} = 1870.0 \qquad b_0 = 17847.87 - 1870.0 \cdot 3.847 = 10654.7 \qquad \hat{y} = 10654.7 + 1870.0x$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: ŷ = 10,654.7 + 1870.0x; one extra star brings about 1,870 extra downloads.'),
 ('p', '**c)** Multiple R = 0.629 and R Square = 0.396. Multiple R is |r|, the strength of the relationship; here r is also positive because the slope is positive. Adjusted R Square = 0.350 is smaller because it corrects R² for the number of predictors and the sample size: with only 15 apps, part of the 0.396 is just chance.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: r = 0.629, R² = 0.396 (39.6% of the variation is explained), Adjusted R² = 0.350.'),
 ('p', '**d)** The Standard Error s = 1718.2: a typical prediction is wrong by about 1,700 downloads. For a rating of 4.5:'),
 ('eq', r'$$\hat{y} = 10654.7 + 1870.0 \cdot 4.5 = 19069.6 \qquad \hat{y} \pm 2s = 19069.6 \pm 3436.4 = [15633,\ 22506]$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: about 19,070 downloads, roughly between 15,600 and 22,500.'),
 ('p', '**e)** The slope has t Stat = 2.92 and P-value = 0.012 < 0.05; the 95% CI is [486, 3254] and does not contain 0. The interval is wide because the points are far from the line (large s) and the sample is small.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: the rating has a significant effect on downloads, but its size is very uncertain (between about 500 and 3,250 downloads per star).'),
 ('p', '**f)** The P-value of the Intercept is 0.0009, so b₀ is significantly different from 0. But b₀ is the prediction for a rating of 0, which is impossible (ratings go from 1 to 5) and far outside the data (2.5 to 4.9).'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: no. A significant intercept only tells us that the line does not pass through the origin; b₀ has no practical meaning here.'),
 ('p', '**g)** The investor is only partly right: the effect is significant, but the rating explains only 40% of the variation.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: other factors include marketing budget, the app’s age, price, the category ranking, store featuring, and the number of reviews. The causality can also go the other way: popular apps get more reviews and may look better.'),
 ('p', '**Excel check:** =SLOPE(…) → 1869.97, =INTERCEPT(…) → 10654.72, =RSQ(…) → 0.3960, =STEYX(…) → 1718.22, =FORECAST.LINEAR(4.5, …) → 19069.58.'),
 ('box', 'mistake', '**Common mistake:** Concluding that a significant slope means a strong relationship. Significance tells us that β₁ ≠ 0; R² tells us how much of Y the line explains.'),
]
S[4] = head('n = 15 employees; X = cups of coffee per day, Y = productivity score; the sums; α = 0.05.',
            'the regression line, the residual output and plot, the ANOVA table, R² and the limitations of the study.',
            'simple linear regression, residuals, and the ANOVA decomposition SST = SSR + SSE.') + [
 ('p', '**a)** X = cups of coffee per day, Y = productivity score. The scatter plot shows a positive linear trend.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: X = coffee, Y = productivity; a positive linear relationship.'),
 ('p', '**b)** The slope and the intercept are:'),
 ('eq', r'$$b_1 = \frac{389.37}{141.33} = 2.755 \qquad b_0 = 71.373 - 2.755 \cdot 4.667 = 58.52 \qquad \hat{y} = 58.52 + 2.755x$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: ŷ = 58.52 + 2.755x; one extra cup brings about 2.76 productivity points.'),
 ('p', '**c)** Employee 2 drinks 4 cups: ŷ = 58.52 + 2.755 · 4 = 69.54, so the residual is e = 77.2 − 69.54 = 7.66. This is the largest residual (furthest above the line). The most negative residual belongs to employee 6 (9 cups, 76.8 points): e = 76.8 − 83.31 = −6.51.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: ŷ₂ = 69.54, e₂ = +7.66; furthest above: employee 2, furthest below: employee 6. The residuals add up to 0, as always for the least squares line.'),
 ('p', '**d)** The residuals form a random cloud around 0, with no curve and no fan shape.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: no clear pattern. The residual plot checks linearity, constant variance (homoscedasticity) and errors independent of X.'),
 ('p', '**e)** The degrees of freedom are: Regression 1 (one predictor), Residual n − 2 = 13 (two estimated coefficients), Total n − 1 = 14. Then:'),
 ('eq', r'$$MSR = \frac{1072.69}{1} = 1072.69 \qquad MSE = \frac{221.10}{13} = 17.008 \qquad F = \frac{1072.69}{17.008} = 63.07$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: F = 63.07, the same as in Excel. MS Residual = 17.008 = s² = 4.1241².'),
 ('p', '**f)** R² = 1072.69 / 1293.79 = 0.829; Significance F = 2.4 · 10⁻⁶ < 0.05.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: R² = 0.829 (82.9% of the variation in productivity is explained by coffee); the relationship is significant.'),
 ('p', '**g)** The sentence is too strong.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: productivity is self-reported (people who drink coffee may simply feel more productive), the study lasted one week with 15 people, and other factors (sleep, workload, job type) can explain both coffee and productivity. Correlation is not causation. 15 cups per day is far outside the data (0 to 9 cups) and also unhealthy, so the model should not be used there.'),
 ('p', '**Excel check:** =SLOPE(…) → 2.7550, =INTERCEPT(…) → 58.5169, =STEYX(…)^2 → 17.008, Regression output: F = 63.07.'),
]
S[5] = head('n = 15 months; X = ad budget in $1000s, Y = new sign-ups; the sums; α = 0.05.',
            'the regression line, the cost per user, t Stat, the meaning and the interval of the intercept, and predictions.',
            'simple linear regression and the coefficients table of the Excel output.') + [
 ('p', '**a)** The slope and the intercept are:'),
 ('eq', r'$$b_1 = \frac{69627.94}{1392.12} = 50.02 \qquad b_0 = 1854.13 - 50.02 \cdot 30.78 = 314.65 \qquad \hat{y} = 314.65 + 50.02x$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: ŷ = 314.65 + 50.02x; a strong positive linear relationship.'),
 ('p', '**b)** Every extra $1000 brings about 50 new users, so one user costs about 1000 / 50.02 ≈ $20.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: +50 users per $1000; about $20 per new user.'),
 ('p', '**c)** Slope: t Stat = 50.02 / 3.169 = 15.78. Intercept: t Stat = 314.65 / 102.22 = 3.08.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: the t Stat of the slope is much larger, so the evidence for the effect of the budget is much stronger than the evidence that b₀ ≠ 0.'),
 ('p', '**d)** b₀ = 314.65 is the predicted number of sign-ups with a budget of 0: the “organic” users who find the app without ads. Its P-value is 0.009 < 0.05, and the 95% CI is [93.8, 535.5].'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: we are 95% confident that without ads the app would still get between about 94 and 535 new users per month. This makes sense because a budget of 0 is a realistic situation, while in Task 3 a rating of 0 is impossible.'),
 ('p', '**e)** R² = 0.950. The 95% CI for the slope is [43.2, 56.9].'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: yes. Even the lower limit (43.2) is above 40, so every $1000 brings at least about 43 new users.'),
 ('p', '**f)** ŷ(20) = 314.65 + 50.02 · 20 = 1315; ŷ(50) = 314.65 + 50.02 · 50 = 2815; ŷ(100) = 5316.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: about 1315 users for $20,000 (inside the data, reliable) and 2815 for $50,000 (just outside, less reliable). For $100,000 the model says 5316, but this is far outside the data: the audience is limited and ads become less effective (diminishing returns).'),
 ('p', '**Excel check:** =SLOPE(…) → 50.016, =INTERCEPT(…) → 314.65, =FORECAST.LINEAR(20, …) → 1314.96, =FORECAST.LINEAR(50, …) → 2815.43.'),
]
S[6] = head('n = 15 load tests; X = simultaneous users, Y = response time in ms; the sums; SLA limit 300 ms; α = 0.05.',
            'the regression line, the outlier, the 95% and 99% intervals, the SLA limit and the Black Friday prediction.',
            'simple linear regression, standardized residuals and confidence intervals for β₁.') + [
 ('p', '**a)** The slope and the intercept are:'),
 ('eq', r'$$b_1 = \frac{277350}{700000} = 0.3962 \qquad b_0 = 251.13 - 0.3962 \cdot 400 = 92.65 \qquad \hat{y} = 92.65 + 0.3962x$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: every 100 extra users add about 40 ms; an “empty” server answers in about 93 ms.'),
 ('p', '**b)** In the Line Fit Plot, the observed point for test 10 (500 users, 377 ms) lies far above the predicted value (290.8 ms).'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: test 10.'),
 ('p', '**c)** Test 10 has residual 86.25 and standardized residual 3.12 > 2; all other points are between −1.42 and 0.68. The backup is a known cause that has nothing to do with the number of users, so it is reasonable to remove the point (and report this). Without it: ŷ = 91.75 + 0.3828x and R² = 0.975.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: test 10 is an outlier. Removing it changes b₁ only a little (0.396 → 0.383) but raises R² from 0.911 to 0.975.'),
 ('p', '**d)** 95% CI for β₁: [0.322, 0.470]; 99% CI: [0.293, 0.500].'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: the 99% interval is wider because a higher confidence needs a larger critical value (t₀.₀₀₅,₁₃ = 3.012 instead of 2.160). Neither interval contains 0.'),
 ('p', '**e)** We solve 92.65 + 0.3962x = 300:'),
 ('eq', r'$$x = \frac{300 - 92.65}{0.3962} = 523.3$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: up to about 523 simultaneous users the predicted response time stays below 300 ms.'),
 ('p', '**f)** ŷ(5000) = 92.65 + 0.3962 · 5000 = 2073.7 ms.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: about 2.1 seconds. This is extrapolation far beyond 750 users: real servers often slow down much faster (queues, memory limits) or crash, so the true time could be much worse.'),
 ('p', '**Excel check:** =SLOPE(…) → 0.3962, =INTERCEPT(…) → 92.648, =FORECAST.LINEAR(5000, …) → 2073.7; Standardized Residual of test 10 → 3.12.'),
]
S[7] = head('n = 15 weeks; X = loading time in seconds, Y = conversion rate in %; the sums; α = 0.05.',
            'a one-tailed test for the slope, the sign of r, R², the 3% target and the lost orders.',
            'simple linear regression and the one-tailed t-test for β₁ with df = 13.') + [
 ('p', '**a)** “Slower pages reduce conversion” means a negative slope:'),
 ('eq', r'$$H_0: \beta_1 \ge 0 \qquad H_1: \beta_1 < 0$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: a one-tailed (left-tailed) test.'),
 ('p', '**b)** The slope and the intercept are:'),
 ('eq', r'$$b_1 = \frac{-20.613}{43.593} = -0.4729 \qquad b_0 = 3.047 - (-0.4729) \cdot 3.233 = 4.576 \qquad \hat{y} = 4.576 - 0.4729x$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: every extra second of loading time lowers the conversion rate by about 0.47 percentage points.'),
 ('p', '**c)** With Labels, the output uses the column names (for example “Loading time, s (X)”) instead of “X Variable 1”. Multiple R is always shown as a positive number |r|.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: r = −0.941; the sign comes from the negative slope b₁.'),
 ('p', '**d)** t Stat = −10.00, the two-tailed P-value is 1.8 · 10⁻⁷, so the one-tailed p-value is 9.0 · 10⁻⁸. For a left-tailed test the critical value is −1.771, and −10.00 < −1.771.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: we reject H₀. Slower pages significantly reduce the conversion rate.'),
 ('p', '**e)** R² = 0.885.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: 88.5% of the variation in the conversion rate is explained by the loading time.'),
 ('p', '**f)** We solve 4.576 − 0.4729x = 3:'),
 ('eq', r'$$x = \frac{4.576 - 3}{0.4729} = 3.33 \text{ s}$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: the page must load in at most about 3.3 seconds.'),
 ('p', '**g)** ŷ(2) = 3.630% and ŷ(4) = 2.684%, a drop of 0.946 percentage points. With 10,000 visitors: 363 orders instead of 268.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: about 95 lost orders per day. For 12 seconds the model gives 4.576 − 0.4729 · 12 = −1.10%, which is impossible (a percentage cannot be negative); 12 s is far outside the data (0.8 to 6.5 s).'),
 ('p', '**Excel check:** =SLOPE(…) → −0.4729, =CORREL(…) → −0.9407, =T.INV(0.95, 13) → 1.771, =FORECAST.LINEAR(2, …) → 3.630.'),
 ('box', 'mistake', '**Common mistake:** Using the two-tailed P-value from Excel for a one-tailed claim, or reading Multiple R as r without adding the sign of b₁.'),
]
S[8] = head('n = 15 households; X = household size, Y = monthly grocery spending in $; the sums; α = 0.05.',
            'the usual regression line, the line through the origin, a comparison of the two models and predictions.',
            'simple linear regression with and without an intercept (Constant is Zero).') + [
 ('p', '**a)** The slope and the intercept are:'),
 ('eq', r'$$b_1 = \frac{4752.44}{50.933} = 93.31 \qquad b_0 = 696.72 - 93.31 \cdot 4.267 = 298.61 \qquad \hat{y} = 298.61 + 93.31x$$'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: ŷ = 298.61 + 93.31x.'),
 ('p', '**b)** Each additional household member adds about $93 to the monthly grocery bill.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: +$93 per person. The intercept ($299) is not the spending of an “empty” household; it is the fixed part of the bill that every household has (basic products, bulk items, things shared by everyone).'),
 ('p', '**c)** With Constant is Zero, Excel gives ŷ = 152.29x.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: usual model ŷ = 298.61 + 93.31x; through the origin ŷ = 152.29x. The slope grows by about $59 (63%), because the fixed costs are now pushed into the cost per person.'),
 ('p', '**d)** For one person: the usual model gives 298.61 + 93.31 = 391.92; the model through the origin gives 152.29. The two one-person households spend 445.61 and 368.24.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: the usual model (392) is close to the data; the model through the origin (152) is far too low. The usual model describes the data better.'),
 ('p', '**e)** With Constant is Zero, R Square is calculated around 0 (it is about 0.971 here), not around ȳ, so it is not comparable with 0.972 from the usual model.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: compare the residuals (and the residual plot) and the predictions for real households. The model through the origin has large systematic residuals for small and large households.'),
 ('p', '**f)** Constant is Zero is justified only when Y must be 0 when X = 0 **and** the data reach close to X = 0.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: for example, the download time of a file of 0 GB is 0, or the cost of 0 litres of fuel is 0. A household always has at least one member, and grocery spending has a fixed part, so the line should not be forced through the origin.'),
 ('p', '**g)** ŷ(2) = 298.61 + 93.31 · 2 = 485.22 and ŷ(6) = 298.61 + 93.31 · 6 = 858.45.'),
 ('ans', 'Answer: about $485 for 2 people and $858 for 6 people. The accuracy depends on the age of the members (children vs adults), income, diet, eating out, prices in the city and discounts.'),
 ('p', '**Excel check:** =SLOPE(…) → 93.307, =INTERCEPT(…) → 298.611, =LINEST(B2:B16, A2:A16, FALSE) → 152.29, =FORECAST.LINEAR(2, …) → 485.22.'),
]

